  "answer_text": "What you're seeing is most likely **limescale**, also known as **mineral deposits** \u2014 and it's completely harmless! Here's a breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### What It Is\nThe white residue you see on glassware is the result of minerals carried through your water, typically called **limescale**. The primary component is **calcium carbonate (CaCO\u2083)**, a naturally occurring mineral that is a key component of hard water \u2014 water that contains high concentrations of minerals like calcium and magnesium.\n\n---\n\n### Why It Happens Even with Filtered Water\nMost municipal and well water contains dissolved calcium (Ca\u00b2\u207a) and magnesium (Mg\u00b2\u207a) ions. When water hardness reaches moderate to high levels, these minerals remain dissolved only as long as the water stays cool and stable. Standard fridge filters are designed primarily to improve taste and remove things like chlorine, but they don't remove all dissolved minerals. The result is the formation of calcium carbonate and related mineral deposits, which adhere to glass surfaces. Once these minerals have precipitated, they are no longer removable by rinsing because they are no longer dissolved in water.\n\n---\n\n### Is It Dangerous?\nNot at all. Higher amounts of mineral deposits usually signify higher levels of water hardness, but limescale is **harmless to human health**.\n\n---\n\n### How to Clean It\nYou can make a simple cleaning solution by combining **equal parts vinegar and water**. Submerge the affected glassware in the solution and let it soak overnight for best results. After soaking, rinse with warm water until the limescale is gone.\n\nYou can also do a quick test to confirm it's mineral deposits: place a sample of the residue in a cup of vinegar \u2014 if the particles are from hard water, they will bubble up and dissolve into the vinegar.\n\n---\n\nIn short, it's just the natural minerals in your water slowly depositing on the glass over time \u2014 a sign of hard water, not dirty water!",
